window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["ml-algorithms"]=[{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0001","card":"q-ml-algorithms-001-1-overview-setup-the-scikit-learn-interf","sec":"ml-algorithms-s002","q":"Which topics and libraries does the Applied ML Algorithms course use?","a":"<p>Topics: clustering, PCA, classification (logistic regression, decision trees) and regression (linear regression). Libraries: Pandas, scikit-learn, XGBoost, Jupyter, Matplotlib/Seaborn and UMAP.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0002","card":"q-ml-algorithms-001-1-overview-setup-the-scikit-learn-interf","sec":"ml-algorithms-s002","q":"What are the prerequisites for the Applied ML Algorithms course?","a":"<p>Python basics, Jupyter, and basic statistics and linear algebra (mean, std, matrix multiplication).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0003","card":"q-ml-algorithms-001-1-overview-setup-the-scikit-learn-interf","sec":"ml-algorithms-s002","q":"Which package versions does the Applied ML Algorithms <code>requirements.txt</code> pin?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Text</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pandas==2.2.0\nscikit-learn==1.3.2\nxgboost==2.0.3\nmatplotlib==3.8.2\nseaborn==0.13.1\nnumpy==1.26.3\numap-learn==0.5.5\nyellowbrick==1.5\nplotly==5.18.0</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0004","card":"q-ml-algorithms-001-1-overview-setup-the-scikit-learn-interf","sec":"ml-algorithms-s002","q":"How do you install the Hugging Face <code>datasets</code> library that the Applied ML Algorithms notebooks use?","a":"<p>With <code>pip install datasets</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0005","card":"q-ml-algorithms-001-1-overview-setup-the-scikit-learn-interf","sec":"ml-algorithms-s002","q":"What are the exercise and solution files of the Applied ML Algorithms course?","a":"<p><code>algos.ipynb</code> holds the exercises and <code>soln.ipynb</code> holds the solutions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0006","card":"q-ml-algorithms-001-1-overview-setup-the-scikit-learn-interf","sec":"ml-algorithms-s002","q":"In scikit-learn, what are <code>X</code> and <code>y</code>, and how do you select them from a DataFrame?","a":"<p><code>X</code> (capital) is a 2D matrix (rows = samples, columns = features). <code>y</code> is the label vector (a 1D Series). Use <code>df[['col']]</code> for X and <code>df['col']</code> for y.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0007","card":"q-ml-algorithms-001-1-overview-setup-the-scikit-learn-interf","sec":"ml-algorithms-s002","q":"In scikit-learn, what does an attribute ending in <code>_</code> mean?","a":"<p>It exists only after <code>.fit()</code>. Examples: <code>cluster_centers_</code>, <code>coef_</code>, <code>intercept_</code>, <code>components_</code>, <code>explained_variance_ratio_</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0008","card":"q-ml-algorithms-001-1-overview-setup-the-scikit-learn-interf","sec":"ml-algorithms-s002","q":"Why is the scikit-learn interface convenient when you swap algorithms?","a":"<p>Every estimator has the same interface, so you can swap the class and keep the rest of the code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0009","card":"q-ml-algorithms-001-1-overview-setup-the-scikit-learn-interf","sec":"ml-algorithms-s002","q":"In scikit-learn, which methods do you use for fit, predict, transform and score?","a":"<div class=\"table-wrap\">\n  <table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Used for</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><code>.fit(X)</code></td><td>Unsupervised learning (clustering, PCA)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><code>.fit(X, y)</code></td><td>Supervised learning (regression, classification)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><code>.predict(X)</code></td><td>Predictions (labels or values)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><code>.transform(X)</code> / <code>.fit_transform(X)</code></td><td>Transformers (<code>StandardScaler</code>, <code>PCA</code>)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><code>.score(X, y)</code></td><td>R² (regression) or accuracy (classification)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0010","card":"q-ml-algorithms-002-2-1-k-means","sec":"ml-algorithms-s003","q":"What is K-means clustering?","a":"<p>An unsupervised algorithm: you pass data without labels and get cluster labels back.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0011","card":"q-ml-algorithms-002-2-1-k-means","sec":"ml-algorithms-s003","q":"What are the steps of the K-means algorithm?","a":"<ol>\n  <li>Choose <code>k</code>.</li>\n  <li>Pick <code>k</code> random points as initial centroids.</li>\n  <li>Assign each point to its nearest centroid.</li>\n  <li>Move each centroid to the middle of its cluster.</li>\n  <li>Repeat steps 3-4 until the centroids stop moving (converge).</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0012","card":"q-ml-algorithms-002-2-1-k-means","sec":"ml-algorithms-s003","q":"Why must features be on the same scale for K-means?","a":"<p>K-means uses distance, so features must be on the same scale.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0013","card":"q-ml-algorithms-002-2-1-k-means","sec":"ml-algorithms-s003","q":"How do you load the Iris sepal data for the K-means demo?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import sklearn.datasets\ndataset = sklearn.datasets.load_iris(as_frame=True)\nX = (dataset\n     [&#x27;data&#x27;]\n     .loc[:, [&#x27;sepal length (cm)&#x27;, &#x27;sepal width (cm)&#x27;]]\n)\ny = dataset[&#x27;target&#x27;]</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0014","card":"q-ml-algorithms-002-2-1-k-means","sec":"ml-algorithms-s003","q":"How does the K-means demo show the centroids moving per iteration?","a":"<p>It refits <code>KMeans</code> with <code>max_iter=i+1</code> on each loop iteration, using <code>init='random'</code> and <code>n_init=1</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>model = KMeans(n_clusters=3, init=&#x27;random&#x27;, n_init=1,\n                max_iter=i+1, random_state=42)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0015","card":"q-ml-algorithms-002-2-1-k-means","sec":"ml-algorithms-s003","q":"How do you plot the K-means centers as red stars on a scatter plot?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>centers = model.cluster_centers_\nax.scatter(centers[:, 0], centers[:, 1], marker=&#x27;*&#x27;, s=250, color=&#x27;r&#x27;, alpha=.5)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0016","card":"q-ml-algorithms-003-2-2-choosing-k","sec":"ml-algorithms-s004","q":"What is inertia, and how does it behave as <code>k</code> grows?","a":"<p>Inertia is the sum of squared distances from each point to its nearest centroid. It always falls as <code>k</code> rises.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0017","card":"q-ml-algorithms-003-2-2-choosing-k","sec":"ml-algorithms-s004","q":"How does the elbow method choose K?","a":"<p>Track inertia as <code>k</code> grows and look for the bend where gains become small. Trick: slide an angled ruler along the curve. The corner where it touches is the elbow.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0018","card":"q-ml-algorithms-003-2-2-choosing-k","sec":"ml-algorithms-s004","q":"How do you compute inertia for 1 to 20 clusters?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>inertias = []\nmax_clusters = 20\nfor i in range(max_clusters):\n    km = KMeans(n_clusters=i+1, n_init=&#x27;auto&#x27;, max_iter=300, random_state=42)\n    km.fit(X)\n    inertias.append(km.inertia_)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0019","card":"q-ml-algorithms-003-2-2-choosing-k","sec":"ml-algorithms-s004","q":"How do you plot the elbow curve?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 6))\nax.plot(range(1, max_clusters+1), inertias, marker=&#x27;o&#x27;)\nax.set_xlabel(&#x27;Number of clusters&#x27;)\nax.set_ylabel(&#x27;Inertia&#x27;)\nax.set_title(&#x27;Elbow Method&#x27;)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0020","card":"q-ml-algorithms-003-2-2-choosing-k","sec":"ml-algorithms-s004","q":"What is the silhouette coefficient and what range does it have?","a":"<p>How similar a point is to its own cluster versus other clusters, from -1 to 1 (higher is better). 1 means near the cluster centre, 0 means on the boundary, and -1 means far away or misassigned (likely an outlier).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0021","card":"q-ml-algorithms-003-2-2-choosing-k","sec":"ml-algorithms-s004","q":"How do you draw silhouette plots for 2 to 7 clusters with yellowbrick?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from yellowbrick.cluster import silhouette_visualizer\nfor i in range(6):\n    km = KMeans(n_clusters=i+2, n_init=&#x27;auto&#x27;, max_iter=300, random_state=42)\n    fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 6))\n    viz = silhouette_visualizer(km, X, colors=&#x27;yellowbrick&#x27;, ax=ax, show=False)\n    ax.set_xlim([-0.1, .8])</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0022","card":"q-ml-algorithms-004-2-2-choosing-k","sec":"ml-algorithms-s004","q":"How do you read a silhouette plot?","a":"<p>Each cluster is a \"blade\" and each horizontal slice is one point, sorted by score. The red dashed line is the average score.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0023","card":"q-ml-algorithms-004-2-2-choosing-k","sec":"ml-algorithms-s004","q":"What does a good silhouette plot look like, and what does a bad one look like?","a":"<p>Good: blades bulge out (wide, rounded) and all poke past the average line. Bad: pointy, narrow blades, a cluster that falls below the average, or negative scores at the bottom of a blade.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0024","card":"q-ml-algorithms-004-2-2-choosing-k","sec":"ml-algorithms-s004","q":"What does the silhouette plot show for Iris at different K?","a":"<p>2 clusters gives bulgy blades and the highest average. The average drops at 3, negatives appear at 4-5, and it recovers slightly at 6-7.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0025","card":"q-ml-algorithms-004-2-2-choosing-k","sec":"ml-algorithms-s004","q":"For Iris, what do the elbow method and the silhouette plot suggest for K?","a":"<p>The elbow suggests 3-4 clusters. The silhouette suggests 2.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0026","card":"q-ml-algorithms-004-2-2-choosing-k","sec":"ml-algorithms-s004","q":"Is choosing K a formula, and what should you do when several values score about the same?","a":"<p>No, it is judgment. When several values score about the same, choose the simpler model (fewer clusters). You must be able to explain and justify the K you chose, using domain context as well as the metrics.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0027","card":"q-ml-algorithms-004-2-2-choosing-k","sec":"ml-algorithms-s004","q":"How is the elbow and silhouette analysis run on the Australian electricity data, and why sample?","a":"<p>The same elbow loop runs on <code>X = elec.drop(columns=['class'])</code>. The silhouette loop uses <code>silhouette_visualizer(km, X.sample(1_000, random_state=42), ...)</code>, a 1,000-row sample, because the full run takes about 2 min 20 s.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0028","card":"q-ml-algorithms-004-2-2-choosing-k","sec":"ml-algorithms-s004","q":"Which K was chosen for the Australian electricity data and why?","a":"<ol start=\"5\">\n  <li>The elbow suggested about 5 clusters. Silhouette favoured 4 but 5 was close, so 5 was chosen.</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0029","card":"q-ml-algorithms-005-2-3-interpreting-the-clusters","sec":"ml-algorithms-s005","q":"Why does interpreting clusters matter, and what are the three methods?","a":"<p>Explaining what clusters represent matters more than producing them. The three methods are: summarize by group mean, train a surrogate model, and visualize in 2D/3D.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0030","card":"q-ml-algorithms-005-2-3-interpreting-the-clusters","sec":"ml-algorithms-s005","q":"How does summarizing clusters by group mean work?","a":"<p>Compute the mean of each feature per cluster in pandas. Each column is a cluster and each row a feature. Red is low and blue is high, so you can describe each cluster in business terms (for example, \"cluster 4 is the high-demand, high-price cluster\").</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0031","card":"q-ml-algorithms-005-2-3-interpreting-the-clusters","sec":"ml-algorithms-s005","q":"How do you load the Australian electricity data?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from datasets import load_dataset\nelectricity = load_dataset(&#x27;inria-soda/tabular-benchmark&#x27;, data_files=&#x27;clf_num/electricity.csv&#x27;)\nelec = electricity[&#x27;train&#x27;].to_pandas()\nX = elec.drop(columns=[&#x27;class&#x27;])</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0032","card":"q-ml-algorithms-005-2-3-interpreting-the-clusters","sec":"ml-algorithms-s005","q":"How do you cluster the electricity data into 5 clusters and show group means with colors?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>km = KMeans(n_clusters=5, n_init=&#x27;auto&#x27;, max_iter=300, random_state=42)\nkm.fit(X)\nlabel = km.predict(X)\n(elec\n .assign(cluster=label)\n .groupby(&#x27;cluster&#x27;)\n .agg(&#x27;mean&#x27;, numeric_only=True)\n .T\n .style.background_gradient(cmap=&#x27;RdBu&#x27;, axis=&#x27;columns&#x27;)\n)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0033","card":"q-ml-algorithms-005-2-3-interpreting-the-clusters","sec":"ml-algorithms-s005","q":"What is the surrogate model approach for explaining clusters?","a":"<p>Train a decision tree to predict the cluster labels (supervised: X plus cluster labels), then read the tree to explain the clusters. The tree also shows which features matter most.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0034","card":"q-ml-algorithms-005-2-3-interpreting-the-clusters","sec":"ml-algorithms-s005","q":"How do you read a surrogate decision tree?","a":"<p>Start at the root. Each node asks \"is feature &lt; value?\" (left = yes, right = no). A leaf is the predicted cluster.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0035","card":"q-ml-algorithms-005-2-3-interpreting-the-clusters","sec":"ml-algorithms-s005","q":"What did the surrogate tree show for the electricity clusters?","a":"<p><code>date</code> was the first split. Class 4 = high date/period/demand, and class 2 = low across the board.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0036","card":"q-ml-algorithms-005-2-3-interpreting-the-clusters","sec":"ml-algorithms-s005","q":"How do you train and plot a depth-3 surrogate tree on the cluster labels?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>dt = DecisionTreeClassifier(max_depth=3, random_state=42)\ndt.fit(X, label)  # Supervised: X + cluster labels\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(14, 8))\nclass_names = [str(i) for i in range(5)]\nplot_tree(dt, ax=ax, feature_names=X.columns, class_names=class_names, filled=True, fontsize=10)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0037","card":"q-ml-algorithms-005-2-3-interpreting-the-clusters","sec":"ml-algorithms-s005","q":"Which tools can you use to visualize clusters in 2D or 3D?","a":"<p>PCA, t-SNE or UMAP.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0038","card":"q-ml-algorithms-006-3-1-hierarchical-and-dbscan","sec":"ml-algorithms-s006","q":"What process do you follow for any clustering algorithm?","a":"<p>Choose the algorithm, determine K (if needed), then evaluate and explain. Because of the consistent interface, you can swap the class and keep the fit/predict/label code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0039","card":"q-ml-algorithms-006-3-1-hierarchical-and-dbscan","sec":"ml-algorithms-s006","q":"How does hierarchical clustering work?","a":"<p>Start with every point as its own cluster, repeatedly merge the two closest, and record the merge distance. The dendrogram shows those distances, and you cut at a height to pick the number of clusters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0040","card":"q-ml-algorithms-006-3-1-hierarchical-and-dbscan","sec":"ml-algorithms-s006","q":"What are the pros and cons of hierarchical clustering?","a":"<p>Pro: no K upfront, and the dendrogram shows structure. Con: it is slow, because it starts with one cluster per point, so it scales poorly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0041","card":"q-ml-algorithms-006-3-1-hierarchical-and-dbscan","sec":"ml-algorithms-s006","q":"How does DBSCAN work?","a":"<p>It is density-based, like moving a flashlight around the data. Nearby points keep extending the same cluster. When no more nearby points are found, the cluster ends. Points near nothing are noise/outliers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0042","card":"q-ml-algorithms-006-3-1-hierarchical-and-dbscan","sec":"ml-algorithms-s006","q":"What are the pros and cons of DBSCAN?","a":"<p>Pro: arbitrary shapes (including clusters that wrap around others) and robustness to outliers. Con: you must tune <code>eps</code> (neighbourhood radius) and <code>min_samples</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0043","card":"q-ml-algorithms-006-3-1-hierarchical-and-dbscan","sec":"ml-algorithms-s006","q":"How do K-means, hierarchical clustering and DBSCAN compare?","a":"<div class=\"table-wrap\">\n  <table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Algorithm</th><th>K required?</th><th>Shape</th><th>Speed</th><th>Outlier handling</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><strong>K-means</strong></td><td>Yes</td><td>Spherical</td><td>Fast</td><td>Poor</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Hierarchical</strong></td><td>No (dendrogram)</td><td>Any</td><td>Slow</td><td>Moderate</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>DBSCAN</strong></td><td>No</td><td>Arbitrary</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Good (labels noise)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0044","card":"q-ml-algorithms-007-3-2-challenge-k-means-on-titanic-data-pr","sec":"ml-algorithms-s007","q":"What is the Titanic K-means challenge task?","a":"<p>Drop missing values, drop categorical features, scale, run K-means, and use the elbow method.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0045","card":"q-ml-algorithms-007-3-2-challenge-k-means-on-titanic-data-pr","sec":"ml-algorithms-s007","q":"How do you load the Titanic data?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import pandas as pd\nurl = &#x27;https://github.com/mattharrison/datasets/raw/master/data/titanic3.xls&#x27;\nraw = pd.read_excel(url)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0046","card":"q-ml-algorithms-007-3-2-challenge-k-means-on-titanic-data-pr","sec":"ml-algorithms-s007","q":"How do you check Titanic missing values and data types, and what do you find?","a":"<p>Use <code>raw.isna().sum()</code> and <code>raw.dtypes</code>. Age has about 263 missing and cabin about 1014, and boat and body have many missing. Drop non-numeric (object) columns.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>raw.isna().sum()\nraw.dtypes</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0047","card":"q-ml-algorithms-007-3-2-challenge-k-means-on-titanic-data-pr","sec":"ml-algorithms-s007","q":"Why select the columns you want instead of dropping the ones you don't?","a":"<p>Positive selection is clearer and less error-prone.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0048","card":"q-ml-algorithms-007-3-2-challenge-k-means-on-titanic-data-pr","sec":"ml-algorithms-s007","q":"What does the <code>tweak_titanic</code> function do?","a":"<p>It keeps the numeric columns <code>pclass</code>, <code>survived</code>, <code>age</code>, <code>sibsp</code>, <code>parch</code> and <code>fare</code>, then drops rows with missing values.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def tweak_titanic(df):\n    return (df\n            .loc[:, [&#x27;pclass&#x27;, &#x27;survived&#x27;, &#x27;age&#x27;, &#x27;sibsp&#x27;, &#x27;parch&#x27;, &#x27;fare&#x27;]]\n            .dropna()\n    )</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0049","card":"q-ml-algorithms-007-3-2-challenge-k-means-on-titanic-data-pr","sec":"ml-algorithms-s007","q":"How do you verify that <code>tweak_titanic</code> removed all missing values?","a":"<p><code>.isna().sum()</code> should show all zeros.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0050","card":"q-ml-algorithms-007-3-2-challenge-k-means-on-titanic-data-pr","sec":"ml-algorithms-s007","q":"Why standardize the Titanic data before K-means?","a":"<p>Clustering uses distance. <code>fare</code> reaches about 150 and <code>age</code> about 80, while other columns are 0-3, so large-range features would dominate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0051","card":"q-ml-algorithms-007-3-2-challenge-k-means-on-titanic-data-pr","sec":"ml-algorithms-s007","q":"What does <code>set_config(transform_output='pandas')</code> do?","a":"<p>It makes transformers return DataFrames instead of NumPy arrays.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0052","card":"q-ml-algorithms-007-3-2-challenge-k-means-on-titanic-data-pr","sec":"ml-algorithms-s007","q":"How do you scale the Titanic data and what is the result?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>scaler = StandardScaler()\nX = scaler.fit_transform(tweak_titanic(raw))</code></pre></div>\n<p>Every column then has mean about 0 and std about 1.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0053","card":"q-ml-algorithms-007-3-2-challenge-k-means-on-titanic-data-pr","sec":"ml-algorithms-s007","q":"How do you run the elbow method on the scaled Titanic data?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>inertias = []\nfor i in range(20):\n    km = KMeans(n_clusters=i+1, n_init=1, max_iter=300, random_state=42)\n    km.fit(X)\n    inertias.append(km.inertia_)\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 6))\nax.plot(range(1, 21), inertias, marker=&#x27;o&#x27;)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0054","card":"q-ml-algorithms-007-3-2-challenge-k-means-on-titanic-data-pr","sec":"ml-algorithms-s007","q":"How many clusters does the Titanic elbow suggest, and what if the ruler touches several spots?","a":"<p>About 5 clusters by the ruler technique. If the ruler touches several spots, pick the simpler one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0055","card":"q-ml-algorithms-008-4-pca-and-other-dimensionality-reduction","sec":"ml-algorithms-s008","q":"What is PCA?","a":"<p>An unsupervised dimensionality reduction technique. It finds linear combinations of the original features that capture the most variance. Each is a principal component (PC).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0056","card":"q-ml-algorithms-008-4-pca-and-other-dimensionality-reduction","sec":"ml-algorithms-s008","q":"What are the use cases of PCA?","a":"<p>Visualizing high-dimensional data in 2D/3D, cutting columns to speed up other algorithms, and finding which features carry the information.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0057","card":"q-ml-algorithms-008-4-pca-and-other-dimensionality-reduction","sec":"ml-algorithms-s008","q":"What does PCA assume?","a":"<p>Linear relationships. PC1 will not always hold 90%+ of the variance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0058","card":"q-ml-algorithms-009-4-1-running-pca","sec":"ml-algorithms-s009","q":"How do you run PCA with 2 components on two Iris features?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pca = PCA(n_components=2)\npca.fit(X)\nX_pca = pca.transform(X)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0059","card":"q-ml-algorithms-009-4-1-running-pca","sec":"ml-algorithms-s009","q":"How do you plot a 2-component PCA result colored by Iris class?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 6))\nax.scatter(X_pca[&#x27;pca0&#x27;], X_pca[&#x27;pca1&#x27;], c=y, cmap=&#x27;viridis&#x27;)\nax.set_xlabel(&#x27;PC1&#x27;)\nax.set_ylabel(&#x27;PC2&#x27;)\nax.set_title(&#x27;PCA on Iris Data&#x27;)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0060","card":"q-ml-algorithms-009-4-1-running-pca","sec":"ml-algorithms-s009","q":"How do you run PCA on all four Iris features?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pca = PCA()\nX_all = (dataset\n     [&#x27;data&#x27;]\n)\npca.fit(X_all)\nX_pca = pca.transform(X_all)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0061","card":"q-ml-algorithms-009-4-1-running-pca","sec":"ml-algorithms-s009","q":"What does scikit-learn call PCA components, and how can you rename them?","a":"<p>scikit-learn names components <code>pca0</code>, <code>pca1</code>, ... while the literature says PC1, PC2. Rename with a function.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def rename_pc0_to_PC1(col):\n    num = int(col[3:]) + 1\n    return &#x27;PC&#x27; + str(num)\nX_pca.rename(columns=rename_pc0_to_PC1)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0062","card":"q-ml-algorithms-010-4-2-the-three-outputs-of-pca","sec":"ml-algorithms-s010","q":"What are the three outputs of PCA?","a":"<ol>\n  <li>Principal components: the transformed data, with the same rows but PCs as columns.</li>\n  <li>Explained variance ratio: information per PC, in descending order.</li>\n  <li>Feature weights (<code>components_</code>): how much each original feature contributes to each PC.</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0063","card":"q-ml-algorithms-010-4-2-the-three-outputs-of-pca","sec":"ml-algorithms-s010","q":"What is the explained variance ratio of the four Iris PCs?","a":"<p>About 0.9246, 0.0531, 0.0171 and 0.0052. PC1 has 92.5%, PC2 5.3%, PC3 1.7% and PC4 0.5%.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pca.explained_variance_ratio_\n# [0.9246, 0.0531, 0.0171, 0.0052]</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0064","card":"q-ml-algorithms-010-4-2-the-three-outputs-of-pca","sec":"ml-algorithms-s010","q":"How do you draw a scree plot?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 6))\nax.plot(range(1, len(pca.explained_variance_ratio_)+1),\n        pca.explained_variance_ratio_, marker=&#x27;o&#x27;)\nax.set_xlabel(&#x27;Principal Component&#x27;)\nax.set_ylabel(&#x27;Explained Variance Ratio&#x27;)\nax.set_title(&#x27;Scree Plot&#x27;)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0065","card":"q-ml-algorithms-010-4-2-the-three-outputs-of-pca","sec":"ml-algorithms-s010","q":"How do you use the cumulative explained variance to choose how many PCs to keep?","a":"<p>The cumulative sum shows the cutoff. For Iris, PC1 alone is 92%, PC1+PC2 is 97.8% and all 4 are 100%. To keep 95% of the information, take the first 2 PCs and drop the rest. Fewer columns make many ML algorithms much faster.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pca.explained_variance_ratio_.cumsum()\n# [0.9246, 0.9777, 0.9948, 1.0000]</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0066","card":"q-ml-algorithms-011-4-2-the-three-outputs-of-pca","sec":"ml-algorithms-s010","q":"How do you read PCA feature weights?","a":"<p>Large magnitude means the feature strongly affects the PC. Near 0 means it barely matters. If features share a scale, the weights act as feature importance. Otherwise standardize first. The PCs are mutually orthogonal.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0067","card":"q-ml-algorithms-011-4-2-the-three-outputs-of-pca","sec":"ml-algorithms-s010","q":"How do you put the PCA feature weights in a DataFrame?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>components = pd.DataFrame(pca.components_, columns=X.columns,\n                          index=[&#x27;PC1&#x27;, &#x27;PC2&#x27;, &#x27;PC3&#x27;, &#x27;PC4&#x27;])\ncomponents</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0068","card":"q-ml-algorithms-011-4-2-the-three-outputs-of-pca","sec":"ml-algorithms-s010","q":"What are the Iris PC1 and PC2 feature weights, and what do they mean?","a":"<p>PC1 is 0.36 for sepal length, -0.08 for sepal width, 0.86 for petal length and 0.36 for petal width, so PC1 is mostly petal length. PC2 is 0.66, 0.73, -0.17 and -0.08, so PC2 is mostly sepal width and sepal length.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0069","card":"q-ml-algorithms-011-4-2-the-three-outputs-of-pca","sec":"ml-algorithms-s010","q":"How is a principal component value computed by hand?","a":"<p>Centre each feature, multiply it by its weight, and sum. It matches the <code>pca.transform(X)</code> output.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># Manual calculation of PC1 for first row\n-.743333 * .3613 + 0.4426 * -0.0845 + -2.358 * 0.8566 + -0.9993 * 0.3582</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0070","card":"q-ml-algorithms-011-4-2-the-three-outputs-of-pca","sec":"ml-algorithms-s010","q":"How do you do PCA by hand with NumPy?","a":"<p>Eigenvalues are the explained variance, eigenvectors are the components, and PCs are the centred data dotted with the components.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>nums = X_all - X_all.mean()\nvals, vecs = np.linalg.eig(nums.cov())\nidxs = pd.Series(vals).argsort()\nexplained_variance = pd.Series(sorted(vals, reverse=True))</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0071","card":"q-ml-algorithms-011-4-2-the-three-outputs-of-pca","sec":"ml-algorithms-s010","q":"How do you build the component matrix and PCs after the NumPy eigen decomposition?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>comps = (pd.DataFrame(vecs, index=nums.columns)\n .iloc[:, idxs[::-1]]\n .pipe(set_columns)\n)\npcas = (nums.dot(comps))\npcas</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0072","card":"q-ml-algorithms-012-4-3-3d-scatter-with-plotly","sec":"ml-algorithms-s011","q":"How do you draw an interactive 3D scatter of the first three PCs with Plotly?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import plotly.express as px\nfig = px.scatter_3d(pcas, x=&#x27;PC1&#x27;, y=&#x27;PC2&#x27;, z=&#x27;PC3&#x27;, color=y)\nfig.show()</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0073","card":"q-ml-algorithms-012-4-3-3d-scatter-with-plotly","sec":"ml-algorithms-s011","q":"What does the 3D PCA plot of Iris show?","a":"<p>It is interactive (hover, rotate, zoom). PC1 carries 90%+ of the information, and the flower varieties separate even though PCA never saw the labels.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0074","card":"q-ml-algorithms-013-4-4-t-sne-and-umap","sec":"ml-algorithms-s012","q":"How do PCA, t-SNE and UMAP compare?","a":"<div class=\"table-wrap\">\n  <table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Technique</th><th>Preserves</th><th>In scikit-learn?</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><strong>PCA</strong></td><td>Global variance (linear)</td><td>Yes</td><td>Simple, interpretable weights</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>t-SNE</strong></td><td>Local structure</td><td>Yes</td><td>More complex, results can vary between runs</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>UMAP</strong></td><td>Local + global structure</td><td>No (<code>pip install umap-learn</code>)</td><td>More complex, needs tuning</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0075","card":"q-ml-algorithms-013-4-4-t-sne-and-umap","sec":"ml-algorithms-s012","q":"How do you install UMAP?","a":"<p>With <code>pip install umap-learn</code>. UMAP is not in scikit-learn.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0076","card":"q-ml-algorithms-013-4-4-t-sne-and-umap","sec":"ml-algorithms-s012","q":"How do you run t-SNE with 3 components on Iris and plot it?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from sklearn.manifold import TSNE\ntsne = TSNE(n_components=3, random_state=42)\nX_tsne = tsne.fit_transform(X_all)\nfig = px.scatter_3d(X_tsne, x=&#x27;tsne0&#x27;, y=&#x27;tsne1&#x27;, z=&#x27;tsne2&#x27;, color=y)\nfig.show()</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0077","card":"q-ml-algorithms-013-4-4-t-sne-and-umap","sec":"ml-algorithms-s012","q":"How do you run UMAP with 3 components on Iris and plot it?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import umap\nreducer = umap.UMAP(random_state=42, n_components=3)\nX_umap = pd.DataFrame(reducer.fit_transform(X_all), columns=[&#x27;umap0&#x27;, &#x27;umap1&#x27;, &#x27;umap2&#x27;])\nfig = px.scatter_3d(X_umap, x=&#x27;umap0&#x27;, y=&#x27;umap1&#x27;, z=&#x27;umap2&#x27;, color=y)\nfig.show()</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0078","card":"q-ml-algorithms-013-4-4-t-sne-and-umap","sec":"ml-algorithms-s012","q":"Which other dimensionality reduction option does the course name besides PCA, t-SNE and UMAP?","a":"<p>Autoencoders. The course gives no code for them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0079","card":"q-ml-algorithms-013-4-4-t-sne-and-umap","sec":"ml-algorithms-s012","q":"What is the practical workflow to explain clusters with PCA?","a":"<p>Cluster the high-dimensional data, run PCA on the original data, colour the PCA scatter by cluster label, then inspect the PCA weights to see which features drive the clusters. Unsupervised methods show what is in the data without encoding your bias.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0080","card":"q-ml-algorithms-014-4-5-challenge-pca-on-titanic","sec":"ml-algorithms-s013","q":"What is the PCA on Titanic challenge, and why drop <code>survived</code> from the features?","a":"<p>Run PCA on the numeric Titanic columns and plot the first 3 components in Plotly colored by survival. Drop <code>survived</code> from the features so the target does not leak into PCA.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0081","card":"q-ml-algorithms-014-4-5-challenge-pca-on-titanic","sec":"ml-algorithms-s013","q":"How do you scale the Titanic features and run PCA without the target?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>scaler = StandardScaler()\nX = scaler.fit_transform(tweak_titanic(raw).drop(columns=&#x27;survived&#x27;))\npca = PCA()\nX_pca = pca.fit_transform(X)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0082","card":"q-ml-algorithms-014-4-5-challenge-pca-on-titanic","sec":"ml-algorithms-s013","q":"How do you plot the first three Titanic PCs colored by survival?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>fig = px.scatter_3d(X_pca, x=&#x27;pca0&#x27;, y=&#x27;pca1&#x27;, z=&#x27;pca2&#x27;,\n                    color=raw.loc[X_pca.index][&#x27;survived&#x27;])\nfig.show()</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0083","card":"q-ml-algorithms-014-4-5-challenge-pca-on-titanic","sec":"ml-algorithms-s013","q":"Why align Titanic survival colors with <code>raw.loc[X_pca.index]</code>?","a":"<p><code>raw</code> has more rows than <code>X_pca</code>, because rows were dropped by <code>dropna</code>. Using <code>X_pca.index</code> aligns the rows.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0084","card":"q-ml-algorithms-014-4-5-challenge-pca-on-titanic","sec":"ml-algorithms-s013","q":"What does the Titanic PCA plot show, and what are the next steps?","a":"<p>Unlike Iris, survival is not cleanly separated, which suggests a non-linear relationship (for example, the very young and very old surviving more). PCA does not guarantee clean separation, but it still shows the structure. Next steps: check <code>explained_variance_ratio_</code> and <code>components_</code>, and colour by <code>pclass</code> or age groups.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0085","card":"q-ml-algorithms-015-5-linear-regression","sec":"ml-algorithms-s014","q":"What is linear regression?","a":"<p>A supervised algorithm: you pass <code>X</code> and a numeric <code>y</code>. It fits the line (weights) that minimizes the sum of squared errors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0086","card":"q-ml-algorithms-015-5-linear-regression","sec":"ml-algorithms-s014","q":"What is the linear regression equation, and what do β₀ and β mean?","a":"<p>$y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2 + ... + \\beta_n x_n$. β₀ is the intercept and each β is the weight (slope) of one feature column.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0087","card":"q-ml-algorithms-015-5-linear-regression","sec":"ml-algorithms-s014","q":"What are the formulas for the slope and intercept of simple linear regression?","a":"<p>$$\\text{slope} = \\frac{\\sum (x_i - \\bar{x})(y_i - \\bar{y})}{\\sum (x_i - \\bar{x})^2} \\qquad \\text{intercept} = \\bar{y} - \\text{slope} \\times \\bar{x}$$</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0088","card":"q-ml-algorithms-016-5-1-by-hand-on-anscombe-s-quartet","sec":"ml-algorithms-s015","q":"What is Anscombe's quartet?","a":"<p>A classic dataset for visualization. It has x, y1, y2, y3, x4 and y4 columns.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0089","card":"q-ml-algorithms-016-5-1-by-hand-on-anscombe-s-quartet","sec":"ml-algorithms-s015","q":"How do you create the Anscombe's quartet DataFrame?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>x = [10, 8, 13, 9, 11, 14, 6, 4, 12, 7, 5]\ny1 = [8.04, 6.95, 7.58, 8.81, 8.33, 9.96, 7.24, 4.26, 10.84, 4.82, 5.68]\nanscombe = (pd.DataFrame({&#x27;x&#x27;: x, &#x27;y1&#x27;: y1, &#x27;y2&#x27;: y2, &#x27;y3&#x27;: y3, &#x27;x4&#x27;: x4, &#x27;y4&#x27;: y4}))</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0090","card":"q-ml-algorithms-016-5-1-by-hand-on-anscombe-s-quartet","sec":"ml-algorithms-s015","q":"How do you compute the slope and intercept by hand in pandas?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>x1 = anscombe[&#x27;x&#x27;]\ny1 = anscombe[&#x27;y1&#x27;]\nslope = ((x1 - x1.mean()) * (y1 - y1.mean())).sum() / ((x1 - x1.mean())**2).sum()\n# intercept\nintercept = y1.mean() - slope * x1.mean()</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0091","card":"q-ml-algorithms-016-5-1-by-hand-on-anscombe-s-quartet","sec":"ml-algorithms-s015","q":"How do you draw the by-hand regression line over the scatter?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>x1 = np.linspace(4, 14, 100)\ny1 = slope * x1 + intercept\nax.plot(x1, y1, color=&#x27;r&#x27;)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0092","card":"q-ml-algorithms-016-5-1-by-hand-on-anscombe-s-quartet","sec":"ml-algorithms-s015","q":"How does the by-hand calculation generalize beyond one feature?","a":"<p>It generalizes from 1 to N dimensions. Each column gets its own weight.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0093","card":"q-ml-algorithms-017-5-2-with-scikit-learn","sec":"ml-algorithms-s016","q":"How do you fit a linear regression on Anscombe's x and y1 with scikit-learn?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>x1 = anscombe[[&#x27;x&#x27;]]   # Double brackets → DataFrame (not Series)\ny1 = anscombe[&#x27;y1&#x27;]     # Single brackets → Series\nlr1 = LinearRegression()\nlr1.fit(x1, y1)         # Supervised: pass X and y</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0094","card":"q-ml-algorithms-017-5-2-with-scikit-learn","sec":"ml-algorithms-s016","q":"Which attributes hold the slope and intercept, and what values do they have on Anscombe y1?","a":"<p><code>coef_</code> holds the slope (0.5) and <code>intercept_</code> holds the intercept (3.0). Both are learned attributes.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>lr1.coef_       # [0.5]  — slope (learned attribute)\nlr1.intercept_  # 3.0    — intercept (learned attribute)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0095","card":"q-ml-algorithms-017-5-2-with-scikit-learn","sec":"ml-algorithms-s016","q":"Why use double brackets for X and single brackets for y in <code>anscombe</code>?","a":"<p>Double brackets give a DataFrame (2D, as X must be). Single brackets give a Series (1D, for y).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0096","card":"q-ml-algorithms-018-5-3-why-visualization-matters-anscombe","sec":"ml-algorithms-s017","q":"What do Anscombe datasets y1, y2 and y3 look like, and is linear regression appropriate?","a":"<div class=\"table-wrap\">\n  <table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Dataset</th><th>Shape</th><th>Linear regression appropriate?</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>y1</td><td>Scattered around a line</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>y2</td><td>Parabola/curve</td><td>No, the data is curved</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>y3</td><td>Line with one outlier</td><td>Maybe; investigate the outlier first</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0097","card":"q-ml-algorithms-018-5-3-why-visualization-matters-anscombe","sec":"ml-algorithms-s017","q":"What is the lesson of Anscombe's quartet?","a":"<p>All three have the same summary statistics yet look completely different. Always visualize your data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0098","card":"q-ml-algorithms-018-5-3-why-visualization-matters-anscombe","sec":"ml-algorithms-s017","q":"How do you fit linear regressions on Anscombe y2 and y3?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>y2 = anscombe[&#x27;y2&#x27;]\ny3 = anscombe[&#x27;y3&#x27;]\nlr2 = LinearRegression()\nlr2.fit(x1, y2)\nlr3 = LinearRegression()\nlr3.fit(x1, y3)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0099","card":"q-ml-algorithms-019-5-4-real-example-aircraft-elevators-f-16","sec":"ml-algorithms-s018","q":"What does <code>LinearRegression</code> need from the data?","a":"<p>Numeric data with no missing values. The aircraft elevators dataset is pre-processed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0100","card":"q-ml-algorithms-019-5-4-real-example-aircraft-elevators-f-16","sec":"ml-algorithms-s018","q":"How do you load the aircraft elevators data and fit linear regression?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>elevators = load_dataset(&#x27;inria-soda/tabular-benchmark&#x27;, data_files=&#x27;reg_num/elevators.csv&#x27;)\nelev = elevators[&#x27;train&#x27;].to_pandas()\nX = elev.drop(columns=[&#x27;Goal&#x27;])\ny = elev[&#x27;Goal&#x27;]\nlr_elev = LinearRegression()\nlr_elev.fit(X, y)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0101","card":"q-ml-algorithms-019-5-4-real-example-aircraft-elevators-f-16","sec":"ml-algorithms-s018","q":"How does linear regression make a prediction?","a":"<p>The prediction is the sum of (feature × weight) plus the intercept.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0102","card":"q-ml-algorithms-019-5-4-real-example-aircraft-elevators-f-16","sec":"ml-algorithms-s018","q":"How do you read a bar chart of linear regression coefficients?","a":"<p>Larger magnitude means more impact, the sign gives the direction, and near zero means little effect.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pd.Series(lr_elev.coef_, index=X.columns).sort_values().plot.barh(figsize=(8, 6))</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0103","card":"q-ml-algorithms-019-5-4-real-example-aircraft-elevators-f-16","sec":"ml-algorithms-s018","q":"What types are <code>coef_</code> and <code>intercept_</code> for a multi-feature linear regression?","a":"<p><code>coef_</code> is a NumPy array with one weight per feature. <code>intercept_</code> is a single value.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0104","card":"q-ml-algorithms-019-5-4-real-example-aircraft-elevators-f-16","sec":"ml-algorithms-s018","q":"How do you get R² and the other metrics for the elevators linear regression?","a":"<p>R² is the proportion of variance explained (0 to 1, higher is better).</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>lr_elev.score(X, y)\nfrom sklearn.metrics import mean_absolute_error, mean_squared_error\nmean_absolute_error(y, lr_elev.predict(X))   # Average |error|\nmean_squared_error(y, lr_elev.predict(X))    # Average error² (penalizes outliers)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0105","card":"q-ml-algorithms-019-5-4-real-example-aircraft-elevators-f-16","sec":"ml-algorithms-s018","q":"How do you predict for the first elevators row and compare with the actual value?","a":"<p>The prediction is 0.030 and the actual value is 0.031.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>lr_elev.predict(X.iloc[[0]])  # Predict for first row → 0.030\ny.iloc[0]                      # Actual value → 0.031</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0106","card":"q-ml-algorithms-019-5-4-real-example-aircraft-elevators-f-16","sec":"ml-algorithms-s018","q":"How do R², MAE and MSE differ in what they measure?","a":"<div class=\"table-wrap\">\n  <table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Measures</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><strong>R²</strong></td><td>Variance explained</td><td>0-1, higher is better</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>MAE</strong></td><td>Average absolute error</td><td>Negatives don't cancel positives</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>MSE</strong></td><td>Average squared error</td><td>Penalizes large errors more</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0107","card":"q-ml-algorithms-019-5-4-real-example-aircraft-elevators-f-16","sec":"ml-algorithms-s018","q":"What is a good regression score?","a":"<p>It depends on the use case: 0.99 may not suffice for one business and 0.2 may work for another. Between two models on the same data, higher R² wins.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0108","card":"q-ml-algorithms-020-5-5-standardization-same-score-comparabl","sec":"ml-algorithms-s019","q":"How do you standardize the elevators features and refit linear regression?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>scaler = StandardScaler()\nX_scaled = scaler.fit_transform(X)\nlr_std = LinearRegression()\nlr_std.fit(X_scaled, y)\nlr_std.score(X_scaled, y)  # 0.8134 — same as unscaled!</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0109","card":"q-ml-algorithms-020-5-5-standardization-same-score-comparabl","sec":"ml-algorithms-s019","q":"What changes and what stays the same after standardizing linear regression features?","a":"<p>R² stays at about 0.81, but the feature ranking changes. <code>diffRollRate</code> and <code>absRoll</code> jump above <code>SaTime3</code>, which only looked most important because of its scale.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0110","card":"q-ml-algorithms-020-5-5-standardization-same-score-comparabl","sec":"ml-algorithms-s019","q":"When should you standardize for linear regression?","a":"<p>Standardize if you want to interpret which features matter.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0111","card":"q-ml-algorithms-020-5-5-standardization-same-score-comparabl","sec":"ml-algorithms-s019","q":"What does <code>.describe()</code> on the scaled X need?","a":"<p><code>set_config(transform_output='pandas')</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># Verify: mean ≈ 0, std ≈ 1\nX_scaled.describe()</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0112","card":"q-ml-algorithms-021-5-6-compare-with-xgboost","sec":"ml-algorithms-s020","q":"How do you fit XGBoost on the elevators data and what R² does it get?","a":"<p>R² is 0.95, much better than the 0.81 of linear regression.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from xgboost import XGBRegressor\nxgb = XGBRegressor()\nxgb.fit(X, y)\nxgb.score(X, y)  # 0.95 — much better than 0.81</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0113","card":"q-ml-algorithms-021-5-6-compare-with-xgboost","sec":"ml-algorithms-s020","q":"Why does XGBoost beat linear regression on the elevators data?","a":"<p>XGBoost captures non-linear relationships, and it shares the same <code>fit/score/predict</code> interface. Whether 0.95 is enough depends on the business.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0114","card":"q-ml-algorithms-021-5-6-compare-with-xgboost","sec":"ml-algorithms-s020","q":"What are the R² scores of linear regression and XGBoost on the elevators data?","a":"<p>Linear Regression (scaled or not) 0.81, XGBoost 0.95.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0115","card":"q-ml-algorithms-022-5-7-assumptions","sec":"ml-algorithms-s021","q":"How is linear regression often used in practice?","a":"<p>As a baseline, then replaced by something like XGBoost if needed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0116","card":"q-ml-algorithms-022-5-7-assumptions","sec":"ml-algorithms-s021","q":"What are the four assumptions of linear regression and what happens if each is violated?","a":"<div class=\"table-wrap\">\n  <table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Assumption</th><th>Meaning</th><th>If violated</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><strong>Linear relationship</strong></td><td>Straight-line relation between features and target</td><td>Line fits curved data (Anscombe y2); may still be good enough</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>No multicollinearity</strong></td><td>Features aren't correlated</td><td>Correlated features split weight and ruin interpretation</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Homoscedasticity</strong></td><td>Constant error variance across target values</td><td>May need better features or another model</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>No outliers</strong></td><td>No extreme points</td><td>Outliers pull the line (Anscombe y3)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0117","card":"q-ml-algorithms-022-5-7-assumptions","sec":"ml-algorithms-s021","q":"Can you use a linear regression with violated assumptions?","a":"<p>Yes, the model may still be good enough for the business. Judge by whether it saves or makes money.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0118","card":"q-ml-algorithms-023-5-8-challenge-predict-titanic-fare","sec":"ml-algorithms-s022","q":"How do you prepare the Titanic data to predict fare?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>X = tweak_titanic(raw).drop(columns=[&#x27;fare&#x27;])\ny = tweak_titanic(raw)[&#x27;fare&#x27;]</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0119","card":"q-ml-algorithms-023-5-8-challenge-predict-titanic-fare","sec":"ml-algorithms-s022","q":"How do you compare linear regression and XGBoost for the Titanic fare?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>lr = LinearRegression()\nlr.fit(X, y)\nlr.score(X, y)  # 0.38\n# XGBoost comparison\nxgb = XGBRegressor()\nxgb.fit(X, y)\nxgb.score(X, y)  # Much higher</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0120","card":"q-ml-algorithms-023-5-8-challenge-predict-titanic-fare","sec":"ml-algorithms-s022","q":"What is the caveat of the Titanic fare scores?","a":"<p>They are scored on the training data, which a model can simply memorize. The correct approach is a train/test split.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0121","card":"q-ml-algorithms-024-6-logistic-regression","sec":"ml-algorithms-s023","q":"What is logistic regression, despite its name?","a":"<p>A classification algorithm. It fits a sigmoid (S-shaped) curve and outputs a probability. Above 0.5 predicts class 1, below predicts class 0.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0122","card":"q-ml-algorithms-024-6-logistic-regression","sec":"ml-algorithms-s023","q":"What is the sigmoid formula used in logistic regression?","a":"<p>$$y = \\frac{1}{1 + e^{-z}}$$ <code>z</code> is the linear combination of features (as in linear regression) and <code>y</code> is the probability.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0123","card":"q-ml-algorithms-024-6-logistic-regression","sec":"ml-algorithms-s023","q":"How do you plot the sigmoid curve in NumPy?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>x = np.linspace(-10, 10, 100)\ny = 1 / (1 + np.exp(-x))\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 6))\nax.plot(x, y)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0124","card":"q-ml-algorithms-025-6-1-basic-1d-example","sec":"ml-algorithms-s024","q":"How do you fit a logistic regression on a small 1D dataset?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>log_r = LogisticRegression()\nlog_r.fit(log_data[[&#x27;x&#x27;]], log_data[&#x27;y&#x27;])  # X must be 2D (DataFrame)\nlog_r.coef_       # Weight for x\nlog_r.intercept_  # Bias term</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0125","card":"q-ml-algorithms-025-6-1-basic-1d-example","sec":"ml-algorithms-s024","q":"How do you plot the fitted sigmoid over the 1D data?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>x = np.linspace(-3, 4, 100)\ny = 1 / (1 + np.exp(-(log_r.coef_[0][0] * x + log_r.intercept_[0])))</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0126","card":"q-ml-algorithms-025-6-1-basic-1d-example","sec":"ml-algorithms-s024","q":"What do <code>log_r.predict([[-.3]])</code> and <code>log_r.predict([[-.43]])</code> return in the 1D example?","a":"<p><code>-.3</code> returns 1 (right of the 0.5 threshold). <code>-.43</code> returns 0 (left of the threshold).</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>log_r.predict([[-.3]])   # → 1 (right of the 0.5 threshold)\nlog_r.predict([[-.43]])  # → 0 (left of the threshold)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0127","card":"q-ml-algorithms-025-6-1-basic-1d-example","sec":"ml-algorithms-s024","q":"What is the decision boundary in logistic regression?","a":"<p>The point where the sigmoid crosses 0.5. Some misclassifications are expected.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0128","card":"q-ml-algorithms-026-6-2-real-example-eye-movements-22-featur","sec":"ml-algorithms-s025","q":"Why standardize the eye movements features before logistic regression?","a":"<p>Curve-fitting algorithms need same-scale data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0129","card":"q-ml-algorithms-026-6-2-real-example-eye-movements-22-featur","sec":"ml-algorithms-s025","q":"How do you fit logistic regression on the eye movements data and what accuracy does it get?","a":"<p>Accuracy is 0.56.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>X = eye_df.drop(columns=[&#x27;label&#x27;])\ny = eye_df[&#x27;label&#x27;]\n# Standardize — curve-fitting algorithms need same-scale data\nstd = StandardScaler()\nX_scaled = std.fit_transform(X)\neye_log = LogisticRegression()\neye_log.fit(X_scaled, y)\neye_log.score(X_scaled, y)  # 0.56 accuracy</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0130","card":"q-ml-algorithms-026-6-2-real-example-eye-movements-22-featur","sec":"ml-algorithms-s025","q":"How do you load the eye movements data?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>eye = load_dataset(&#x27;inria-soda/tabular-benchmark&#x27;, data_files=&#x27;clf_num/eye_movements.csv&#x27;)\neye_df = eye[&#x27;train&#x27;].to_pandas()</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0131","card":"q-ml-algorithms-026-6-2-real-example-eye-movements-22-featur","sec":"ml-algorithms-s025","q":"What does <code>.score()</code> return for regression and for classification?","a":"<p>R² for regression and accuracy (% correct) for classification.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0132","card":"q-ml-algorithms-026-6-2-real-example-eye-movements-22-featur","sec":"ml-algorithms-s025","q":"How do you read logistic regression coefficients?","a":"<p>A positive coefficient pushes toward the positive class, a negative one away from it, and larger magnitude means more impact.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pd.Series(eye_log.coef_[0], index=X.columns).sort_values().plot.barh(figsize=(8, 6))</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0133","card":"q-ml-algorithms-026-6-2-real-example-eye-movements-22-featur","sec":"ml-algorithms-s025","q":"What is the accuracy trap?","a":"<p>If fraud is 0.1% of transactions, predicting \"not fraud\" always gets 99.9% accuracy and is useless. Tie accuracy to business metrics.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0134","card":"q-ml-algorithms-027-6-3-challenge-predict-titanic-survival-w","sec":"ml-algorithms-s026","q":"How do you prepare the Titanic data for logistic regression?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>titanic_X = (raw\n    .loc[:, [&#x27;pclass&#x27;, &#x27;age&#x27;, &#x27;sibsp&#x27;, &#x27;parch&#x27;, &#x27;fare&#x27;]]\n    .dropna()\n)\n# Standardize\nstd = StandardScaler()\nX = std.fit_transform(titanic_X)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0135","card":"q-ml-algorithms-027-6-3-challenge-predict-titanic-survival-w","sec":"ml-algorithms-s026","q":"How do you split the Titanic data and score logistic regression on unseen data?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>y = raw.loc[X.index, &#x27;survived&#x27;]\nX_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X, y, random_state=42)\nlr = LogisticRegression()\nlr.fit(X_train, y_train)\nlr.score(X_test, y_test)  # ~0.65 accuracy</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0136","card":"q-ml-algorithms-027-6-3-challenge-predict-titanic-survival-w","sec":"ml-algorithms-s026","q":"How do you compare XGBoost with logistic regression for Titanic survival?","a":"<p>Use <code>XGBClassifier</code>. Its test score is sometimes lower than logistic regression.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from xgboost import XGBClassifier\nxgb = XGBClassifier()\nxgb.fit(X_train, y_train)\nxgb.score(X_test, y_test)  # Sometimes lower than logistic regression!</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0137","card":"q-ml-algorithms-027-6-3-challenge-predict-titanic-survival-w","sec":"ml-algorithms-s026","q":"Does XGBoost always beat logistic regression?","a":"<p>No. With few numeric features, logistic regression (about 65%) can match or beat it. The best model depends on the data and features.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0138","card":"q-ml-algorithms-028-7-decision-trees-random-forest-xgboost","sec":"ml-algorithms-s027","q":"What do decision trees do?","a":"<p>They handle classification or regression by recursively splitting data on feature thresholds to minimize error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0139","card":"q-ml-algorithms-028-7-decision-trees-random-forest-xgboost","sec":"ml-algorithms-s027","q":"What are the advantages of decision trees over linear models?","a":"<p>No scaling needed (they look at one column at a time). They handle missing values and categoricals, depending on implementation (XGBoost does). They are explainable: you can visualize and walk the tree. They capture non-linearity by splitting on the same feature repeatedly (bathtub, S/Z shapes).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0140","card":"q-ml-algorithms-029-7-1-decision-stump-and-depth","sec":"ml-algorithms-s028","q":"What is a decision stump?","a":"<p>A tree of depth 1 that makes one decision. On Anscombe y1: if x ≤ 8.5 predict 5.79, else 8.93.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0141","card":"q-ml-algorithms-029-7-1-decision-stump-and-depth","sec":"ml-algorithms-s028","q":"How do you fit a decision stump on Anscombe's x and y1?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>dt = DecisionTreeRegressor(max_depth=1)\nX = anscombe[[&#x27;x&#x27;]]\ny = anscombe[&#x27;y1&#x27;]\ndt.fit(X, y)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0142","card":"q-ml-algorithms-029-7-1-decision-stump-and-depth","sec":"ml-algorithms-s028","q":"How do you plot a fitted tree in scikit-learn?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 6))\n_ = plot_tree(dt, ax=ax, feature_names=[&#x27;x&#x27;], filled=True, fontsize=10)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0143","card":"q-ml-algorithms-029-7-1-decision-stump-and-depth","sec":"ml-algorithms-s028","q":"How do you plot decision tree predictions for 2 levels and for unlimited levels?","a":"<p>Fit trees with <code>max_depth=2</code> and <code>max_depth=None</code>, predict over <code>np.linspace(4, 14, 100)</code> reshaped to a column, and plot.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>dt2 = DecisionTreeRegressor(max_depth=2)\ndt3 = DecisionTreeRegressor(max_depth=None)\ny1 = dt3.predict(x1.reshape(-1, 1))</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0144","card":"q-ml-algorithms-029-7-1-decision-stump-and-depth","sec":"ml-algorithms-s028","q":"How do tree depths 1, 2-3 and None behave?","a":"<div class=\"table-wrap\">\n  <table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Depth</th><th>Behavior</th><th>Problem</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>1 (stump)</td><td>Too simple, misses patterns</td><td><strong>Underfitting</strong></td></tr>\n      <tr><td>2-3</td><td>Good balance</td><td>Appropriate</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>None (unlimited)</td><td>Memorizes every point</td><td><strong>Overfitting</strong></td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0145","card":"q-ml-algorithms-029-7-1-decision-stump-and-depth","sec":"ml-algorithms-s028","q":"What kind of parameter is <code>max_depth</code>?","a":"<p>A hyperparameter: a lever on model complexity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0146","card":"q-ml-algorithms-030-7-2-real-example-aircraft-elevators","sec":"ml-algorithms-s029","q":"How does a depth-3 decision tree compare with linear regression on the elevators data (train score)?","a":"<p>The tree scores 0.48 and linear regression 0.81.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>dt_elev = DecisionTreeRegressor(max_depth=3)\ndt_elev.fit(X_elev, y_elev)\ndt_elev.score(X_elev, y_elev)  # 0.48</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0147","card":"q-ml-algorithms-030-7-2-real-example-aircraft-elevators","sec":"ml-algorithms-s029","q":"What does the depth-3 elevators tree capture?","a":"<p>It splits on <code>SaTime1</code> several times, capturing non-linear behaviour.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0148","card":"q-ml-algorithms-030-7-2-real-example-aircraft-elevators","sec":"ml-algorithms-s029","q":"How do you plot the elevators decision tree?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(12, 8))\n_ = plot_tree(dt_elev, ax=ax, feature_names=X_elev.columns, filled=True, fontsize=10, precision=4)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0149","card":"q-ml-algorithms-030-7-2-real-example-aircraft-elevators","sec":"ml-algorithms-s029","q":"Why is a depth sweep on training data only misleading?","a":"<p>The tree can memorize the training data, so the score keeps climbing.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>scores = []\nfor i in range(1, 20):\n    dt = DecisionTreeRegressor(max_depth=i)\n    dt.fit(X_elev, y_elev)\n    scores.append(dt.score(X_elev, y_elev))</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0150","card":"q-ml-algorithms-030-7-2-real-example-aircraft-elevators","sec":"ml-algorithms-s029","q":"How do you build a correct validation curve with a train/test split for a decision tree?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X_elev, y_elev, random_state=42)\ntest_scores = []\ntrain_scores = []\nfor i in range(1, 20):\n    dt = DecisionTreeRegressor(max_depth=i)\n    dt.fit(X_train, y_train)\n    test_scores.append(dt.score(X_test, y_test))\n    train_scores.append(dt.score(X_train, y_train))</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0151","card":"q-ml-algorithms-030-7-2-real-example-aircraft-elevators","sec":"ml-algorithms-s029","q":"How do you read the decision tree validation curve?","a":"<p>The train score climbs toward 1.0 as depth grows (memorizing). The test score peaks around depth 10-11, then falls. Left of the peak is underfitting and right of it is overfitting. The peak is the sweet spot.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0152","card":"q-ml-algorithms-030-7-2-real-example-aircraft-elevators","sec":"ml-algorithms-s029","q":"How do you train the best elevators tree and compare it with linear regression on the same split?","a":"<p>The tree at depth 11 gets about 0.70 and linear regression about 0.81.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>dt_elev = DecisionTreeRegressor(max_depth=11)\ndt_elev.fit(X_train, y_train)\ndt_elev.score(X_test, y_test)  # ~0.70</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0153","card":"q-ml-algorithms-030-7-2-real-example-aircraft-elevators","sec":"ml-algorithms-s029","q":"When do trees beat linear regression?","a":"<p>Trees shine when relationships are non-linear. Here linear regression beats the single tree.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0154","card":"q-ml-algorithms-031-7-3-random-forest-bagging-and-xgboost-bo","sec":"ml-algorithms-s030","q":"Why use ensembles of trees?","a":"<p>Single trees overfit. Ensembles combine many trees.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0155","card":"q-ml-algorithms-031-7-3-random-forest-bagging-and-xgboost-bo","sec":"ml-algorithms-s030","q":"What is a Random Forest?","a":"<p>Many trees, each trained on a random subset of columns (and optionally rows). The final answer is the average (regression) or the vote (classification).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0156","card":"q-ml-algorithms-031-7-3-random-forest-bagging-and-xgboost-bo","sec":"ml-algorithms-s030","q":"What is the golf analogy for Random Forest?","a":"<p>You and 40 friends each hit a golf ball once, and the average landing spot is closer to the hole than a typical single hit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0157","card":"q-ml-algorithms-031-7-3-random-forest-bagging-and-xgboost-bo","sec":"ml-algorithms-s030","q":"How do you fit a Random Forest with 100 trees and depth 3?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>rf = RandomForestRegressor(n_estimators=100, max_depth=3)\nrf.fit(X_train, y_train)\nrf.score(X_test, y_test)  # 0.528 at depth 3</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0158","card":"q-ml-algorithms-031-7-3-random-forest-bagging-and-xgboost-bo","sec":"ml-algorithms-s030","q":"How do you sweep Random Forest depth and compare train and test scores?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>test_scores = []\ntrain_scores = []\nfor i in range(1, 20):\n    rf = RandomForestRegressor(n_estimators=100, max_depth=i)\n    rf.fit(X_train, y_train)\n    test_scores.append(rf.score(X_test, y_test))\n    train_scores.append(rf.score(X_train, y_train))</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0159","card":"q-ml-algorithms-031-7-3-random-forest-bagging-and-xgboost-bo","sec":"ml-algorithms-s030","q":"Where does the Random Forest test score flatten, and which depth do you pick?","a":"<p>Around depth 11-13. When several depths score similarly, pick the shallower model.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0160","card":"q-ml-algorithms-031-7-3-random-forest-bagging-and-xgboost-bo","sec":"ml-algorithms-s030","q":"How do you train the final Random Forest on elevators data?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>rf = RandomForestRegressor(n_estimators=100, max_depth=13, random_state=42)\nrf.fit(X_train, y_train)\nrf.score(X_test, y_test)  # 0.82</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0161","card":"q-ml-algorithms-031-7-3-random-forest-bagging-and-xgboost-bo","sec":"ml-algorithms-s030","q":"What is XGBoost's boosting idea?","a":"<p>Train a tree, then train another that corrects the previous tree's errors, and repeat. The prediction is the sum of all trees' contributions. Analogy: hit the golf ball again from where it landed, each swing correcting the last.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0162","card":"q-ml-algorithms-031-7-3-random-forest-bagging-and-xgboost-bo","sec":"ml-algorithms-s030","q":"How do you fit XGBoost on the elevators train and test split?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>xgb = XGBRegressor()\nxgb.fit(X_train, y_train)\nxgb.score(X_test, y_test)  # 0.87</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0163","card":"q-ml-algorithms-031-7-3-random-forest-bagging-and-xgboost-bo","sec":"ml-algorithms-s030","q":"How do the elevators models rank on test R²?","a":"<div class=\"table-wrap\">\n  <table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Score</th><th>Technique</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Decision Tree (depth 11)</td><td>~0.70</td><td>Single tree</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Linear Regression</td><td>~0.81</td><td>Line fitting</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Random Forest (depth 13)</td><td>~0.82</td><td>Bagging</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>XGBoost</td><td>~0.87</td><td>Boosting</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0164","card":"q-ml-algorithms-031-7-3-random-forest-bagging-and-xgboost-bo","sec":"ml-algorithms-s030","q":"How do bagging (Random Forest) and boosting (XGBoost) differ?","a":"<div class=\"table-wrap\">\n  <table>\n    <thead><tr><th></th><th>Bagging (Random Forest)</th><th>Boosting (XGBoost)</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Strategy</td><td>Independent trees on random subsets</td><td>Sequential trees, each correcting errors</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Parallelizable</td><td>Yes</td><td>No (each depends on the previous)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Overfitting risk</td><td>Lower</td><td>Higher (but has regularization)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0165","card":"q-ml-algorithms-031-7-3-random-forest-bagging-and-xgboost-bo","sec":"ml-algorithms-s030","q":"What are the key hyperparameters for Random Forest and boosting trees?","a":"<p><code>max_depth</code> (tree complexity) and <code>n_estimators</code> (number of trees).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0166","card":"q-ml-algorithms-032-7-4-challenge-decision-tree-on-titanic-f","sec":"ml-algorithms-s031","q":"Do trees need standardization on the Titanic data?","a":"<p>No, trees don't need standardization.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0167","card":"q-ml-algorithms-032-7-4-challenge-decision-tree-on-titanic-f","sec":"ml-algorithms-s031","q":"How do you prepare the Titanic data and split it for a decision tree?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>titanic_X = (raw\n    .loc[:, [&#x27;pclass&#x27;, &#x27;age&#x27;, &#x27;sibsp&#x27;, &#x27;parch&#x27;, &#x27;fare&#x27;]]\n    .dropna()\n)\ntitanic_y = raw[&#x27;survived&#x27;].loc[titanic_X.index]\nX_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(titanic_X, titanic_y, random_state=43)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0168","card":"q-ml-algorithms-032-7-4-challenge-decision-tree-on-titanic-f","sec":"ml-algorithms-s031","q":"How do you find the best <code>DecisionTreeClassifier</code> depth on Titanic?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>test_scores = []\ntrain_scores = []\nfor i in range(1, 20):\n    dt = DecisionTreeClassifier(max_depth=i)\n    dt.fit(X_train, y_train)\n    test_scores.append(dt.score(X_test, y_test))\n    train_scores.append(dt.score(X_train, y_train))</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0169","card":"q-ml-algorithms-032-7-4-challenge-decision-tree-on-titanic-f","sec":"ml-algorithms-s031","q":"Which depth is best for the Titanic decision tree and why?","a":"<p>Depth 3, where the test score peaks. It is simple, shows no overfitting, and does well on unseen data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0170","card":"q-ml-algorithms-032-7-4-challenge-decision-tree-on-titanic-f","sec":"ml-algorithms-s031","q":"Why is the Titanic test curve jagged, and what is a more stable estimate?","a":"<p>The split matters (changing <code>random_state</code> changes it). For a more stable estimate use k-fold cross validation: split into k chunks (typically 5), hold out each in turn, train on the rest, and average the scores. The course shows no code for it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0171","card":"q-ml-algorithms-033-8-summary-table-cheat-sheet-key-lessons","sec":"ml-algorithms-s032","q":"How do the algorithms in the course compare in type and key concept?","a":"<div class=\"table-wrap\">\n  <table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Algorithm</th><th>Type</th><th>Key concept</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>K-Means</td><td>Unsupervised</td><td>Clusters by distance to centroids; elbow/silhouette for K</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>PCA</td><td>Unsupervised</td><td>Dimensionality reduction; explained variance; feature weights</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Linear Regression</td><td>Supervised (regression)</td><td>Fit a line; R²; coefficients as importance</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Logistic Regression</td><td>Supervised (classification)</td><td>Sigmoid; 0.5 threshold; accuracy</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Decision Tree</td><td>Supervised (both)</td><td>Threshold splits; under/overfitting; validation curves</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Random Forest</td><td>Supervised (both)</td><td>Bagging: many trees on random subsets</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>XGBoost</td><td>Supervised (both)</td><td>Boosting: sequential error correction</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0172","card":"q-ml-algorithms-033-8-summary-table-cheat-sheet-key-lessons","sec":"ml-algorithms-s032","q":"What is the scikit-learn cheat sheet for unsupervised models?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>model.fit(X)\nmodel.predict(X)           # Cluster labels\nmodel.transform(X)         # Reduced dimensions (PCA)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0173","card":"q-ml-algorithms-033-8-summary-table-cheat-sheet-key-lessons","sec":"ml-algorithms-s032","q":"What is the scikit-learn cheat sheet for supervised models?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>model.fit(X_train, y_train)\nmodel.predict(X_test)      # Predictions\nmodel.score(X_test, y_test) # R² (regression) or accuracy (classification)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0174","card":"q-ml-algorithms-033-8-summary-table-cheat-sheet-key-lessons","sec":"ml-algorithms-s032","q":"Which learned attributes can you read after <code>fit</code>?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>model.coef_                # Weights\nmodel.intercept_           # Intercept\nmodel.cluster_centers_     # Centroids (K-means)\nmodel.components_          # Feature weights (PCA)\nmodel.explained_variance_ratio_  # Variance per PC</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0175","card":"q-ml-algorithms-033-8-summary-table-cheat-sheet-key-lessons","sec":"ml-algorithms-s032","q":"When should you standardize and when not?","a":"<p>Standardize for distance-based and curve-fitting algorithms (K-means, PCA, linear and logistic regression). Don't for trees.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0176","card":"q-ml-algorithms-033-8-summary-table-cheat-sheet-key-lessons","sec":"ml-algorithms-s032","q":"What are the key lessons of the Applied ML Algorithms course?","a":"<p>Always visualize data before and after modeling. Never evaluate on data the model has seen; use a train/test split. Validation curves: sweep a hyperparameter, plot train versus test, and pick the sweet spot. When models score similarly, choose the simpler one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0177","card":"q-ml-algorithms-033-8-summary-table-cheat-sheet-key-lessons","sec":"ml-algorithms-s032","q":"How should you judge whether a model score is good?","a":"<p>It depends on the business case. Higher is better between models, but sufficiency needs business context.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ml-algorithms-f0178","card":"q-ml-algorithms-033-8-summary-table-cheat-sheet-key-lessons","sec":"ml-algorithms-s032","q":"What are the next steps recommended after the Applied ML Algorithms course?","a":"<p>Practice on your own data, take Matt Harrison's Feature Engineering and XGBoost courses, and read \"Effective XGBoost\" by Matt Harrison.</p>","hand":true}];
